<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joséphine de Beauharnais</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1763">1763</a>–<a href="/years/1814">1814</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Empress Joséphine</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joséphine de Beauharnais was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/les-trois-ilets">Trois-Îlets</a> on Martinique into a planter family. Widowed when her husband Alexandre de Beauharnais was guillotined in 1794, she married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> in 1796 and became Empress of the French in 1804, until their divorce in 1810. Her circle helped some German princely houses, such as Hohenzollern-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmaringen">Sigmaringen</a>, survive the mediatisation of 1806.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/josephine-de-beauharnais.jpg" alt="Joséphine de Beauharnais" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Baron François Gérard (1770 - 1837) – Painter (French) Born in Rome. Died in Paris. Details on Google Art Project · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baron_Fran%C3%A7ois_G%C3%A9rard_-_Jos%C3%A9phine_in_coronation_costume_-_Google_Art_Project_(cropped,_Josephine_detail).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Joséphine de Beauharnais</b> was born at Trois-Îlets in 1763.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-martinique">Colony of Martinique</a></li><li><blockquote>Thanks partly to Princess Amalie Zephyrine, a friend of <b>Joséphine de Beauharnais</b>, the principality survived the mediatisation of 1806, joined the Confederation of the Rhine and even gained territory.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-hohenzollern-sigmaringen">Principality of Hohenzollern-Sigmaringen</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/les-trois-ilets">Les Trois-Îlets</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Rueil-Malmaison, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=14.54,-61.03&amp;year=1796" aria-label="Les Trois-Îlets on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2881 1623 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Joséphine de Beauharnais"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3331 1893h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3331 1893h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=14.54,-61.03&amp;year=1796">What was here in 1796? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
